#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:2
msgid "Install from source (Docker)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:4
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:6
msgid ""
"This page describes the build and installation of PyBaMM using a Dockerfile, "
"available on GitHub. Note that this is **not the recommended approach for "
"most users** and should be reserved to people wanting to participate in the "
"development of PyBaMM, or people who really need to use bleeding-edge "
"feature(s) not yet available in the latest released version. If you do not "
"fall in the two previous categories, you would be better off installing "
"PyBaMM using ``pip`` or ``conda``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:9
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:11
msgid ""
"Before you begin, make sure you have Docker installed on your system. You "
"can download and install Docker from the official `Docker website <https://"
"www.docker.com/get-started/>`_. Ensure Docker installation by running:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:14
msgid "docker --version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:19
msgid "Pulling the Docker image"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:21
msgid ""
"Use the following command to pull the PyBaMM Docker image from Docker Hub:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:24
msgid "docker pull pybamm/pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:30
msgid "Running the Docker container"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:32
msgid ""
"Once you have pulled the Docker image, you can run a Docker container with "
"the PyBaMM environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:34
msgid ""
"In your terminal, use the following command to start a Docker container from "
"the pulled image:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:37
msgid "docker run -it pybamm/pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:42
msgid ""
"You will now be inside the Docker container's shell. You can use PyBaMM and "
"its dependencies as if you were in a virtual environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:44
msgid ""
"You can execute PyBaMM-related commands, run tests develop & contribute from "
"the container."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:48
msgid ""
"The default user for the container is ``pybamm`` with ``pybamm`` as "
"password. The user belongs to ``sudoers`` and ``root`` group, so the sudo "
"command can be issued to install additional packages to the container.  "
"After a clean install, ``sudo apt-get update`` should be executed to update "
"the source list. Additional packages can be installed using ``sudo apt-get "
"install [package_name]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:54
msgid "Exiting the Docker container"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:56
msgid "To exit the Docker container's shell, you can simply type:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:58
msgid "exit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:62
msgid "This will return you to your host machine's terminal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:65
msgid "Building Docker image locally from source"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:67
msgid ""
"If you want to build the PyBaMM Docker image locally from the PyBaMM source "
"code, follow these steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:69
msgid ""
"Clone the PyBaMM GitHub repository to your local machine if you haven't "
"already:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:71
msgid "git clone https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM.git"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:75
msgid "Change into the PyBaMM directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:77
msgid "cd PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:81
msgid "Build the Docker image using the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:83
msgid "docker build -t pybamm -f scripts/Dockerfile ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:87
msgid "Once the image is built, you can run a Docker container using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:89
msgid "docker run -it pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:93
msgid ""
"Activate PyBaMM development virtual environment inside docker container "
"using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:95
msgid "source /home/pybamm/venv/bin/activate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:100
msgid "Using Git inside a running Docker container"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:103
msgid ""
"You might require re-configuring git while running the docker container for "
"the first time. You can run ``git config --list`` to ensure if you have "
"desired git configuration already."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:106
msgid "Setting up git configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:108
msgid ""
"git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\n"
"\n"
"git config --global user.email your@mail.com"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:114
msgid "Setting a git remote"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:116
msgid ""
"git remote set-url origin <fork_url>\n"
"\n"
"git remote add upstream https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM\n"
"\n"
"git fetch --all"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:125
msgid "Using Visual Studio Code inside a running Docker container"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:127
msgid ""
"You can easily use Visual Studio Code inside a running Docker container by "
"attaching it directly. This provides a seamless development environment "
"within the container. Here's how:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:129
msgid ""
"Install the \"Docker\" extension from Microsoft in your local Visual Studio "
"Code if it's not already installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:130
msgid ""
"Pull and run the Docker image containing PyBaMM development environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:131
msgid ""
"In your local Visual Studio Code, open the \"Docker\" extension by clicking "
"on the Docker icon in the sidebar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:132
msgid ""
"Under the \"Containers\" section, you'll see a list of running containers. "
"Right-click the running PyBaMM container."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:133
msgid "Select \"Attach Visual Studio Code\" from the context menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-docker.rst:134
msgid ""
"Visual Studio Code will now connect to the container, and a new VS Code "
"window will open up, running inside the container. You can now edit, debug, "
"and work on your code using VS Code as if you were working directly on your "
"local machine."
msgstr ""
